LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation","group":"pages","title":"Alphabet, sounds and stress","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Polish letters and sounds, the letters with marks, and the stress rule: second-to-last syllable. Dzień dobry. Dziękuję.","text":"Polish spelling is far more regular than English: know the letters and you can read any word aloud. There are no silent letters, and the stress almost always falls on the second-to-last syllable. New for English speakers: nine letters with marks (ą, ć, ę, ł, ń, ó, ś, ź, ż) and letter pairs that make one sound (sz, cz, rz, ch, dz, dż). How it works 32 letters (q, v, x only in foreign words): a ą b c ć d e ę f g h i j k l ł m n ń o ó p r s ś t u w y z ź ż Letters that surprise English speakers: Letter Sounds like Example c \"ts\" in cats noc (night) ch, h \"ch\" in Scottish loch chleb (bread), herbata (tea) cz \"ch\" in church czas (time) sz \"sh\" in shop szkoła (school) rz, ż \"s\" in pleasure rzeka (river), żona (wife) j \"y\" in yes jajko (egg) w \"v\" in van woda (water) ł \"w\" in water mały (small) y \"i\" in bit ty (you) ó, u \"oo\" in book ołówek (pencil), ucho (ear) ą nasal \"on\", as in French bon są (they are) ę nasal \"en\"; at the end of a word almost a plain \"e\" ręka (hand), dziękuję (thank you) Soft sounds have two spellings: a mark before a consonant or at the end of a word, an i before a vowel. If the vowel is i itself, just write it: zima (winter), nic (nothing). Mark Before a vowel Sounds like Example ś si a soft \"sh\" (tongue flat, as in sheep) środa, siostra ć ci a soft \"ch\" (as in cheese) cześć, ciocia ź zi a soft \"zh\" źle, zima dź dzi a soft \"j\" (as in jeep) dzień ń ni \"ny\" in canyon koń, nie Stress: the second-to-last syllable. An i before a vowel is not a syllable of its own: Ka-sia has two. When to use it Reading a new word: say every letter or letter pair; an i before a vowel only softens the sound before it. Kraków = Kra-ków. Stress: count syllables from the end. War-sza-wa → war-SZA-wa (Warsaw). Marks change words: sad (orchard) and sąd (court) are different words. rz / ż, ó / u and ch / h sound the same, so learn each word with its spelling. Examples Polish English Stress Dzień dobry. Good morning. / Hello. dzień DO-bry Dziękuję. Thank you. dzię-KU-ję Przepraszam. Excuse me. / Sorry. prze-PRA-szam Warszawa Warsaw war-SZA-wa herbata tea her-BA-ta poniedziałek Monday po-nie-DZIA-łek Good to know At the end of a word b, d, g, w, z sound like p, t, k, f, s: chleb sounds like \"chlep\", Kraków like \"Krakuf\". After p, t, k, ch the pair rz sounds like sz: przepraszam, trzy (three). Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lodz → ✓ Łódź — Lodz is the English spelling; in Polish the marks are part of the letters. ✗ woda said with an English \"w\" → ✓ woda with a \"v\" — Polish w is a v sound (an f at the end of a word). ✗ WAR-sza-wa → ✓ war-SZA-wa — stress the second-to-last syllable, not the first. ✗ rzeka said as \"r\" + \"z\" → ✓ rzeka with the \"s\" of pleasure — rz is one sound, the same as ż. Practice Write the stressed syllable in capitals. te-le-fon her-ba-ta ro-wer Which spelling makes the same sound? ż sounds the same as . ó sounds the same as . Add the missing marks. Dzien dobry. Przepraszam, ktora godzina? (Excuse me, what time is it?) How do you say it? The w in woda sounds like English . Answers te-LE-fon her-BA-ta RO-wer rz u Dzień dobry. Przepraszam, która godzina? v","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-alphabet-pronunciation"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-greetings-introductions","group":"pages","title":"Greet people, say goodbye and introduce yourself","headings":["Key phrases","Dialogue","Culture note","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Dzień dobry or Cześć? Formal and informal greetings, saying your name, Miło mi, and ways to say goodbye.","text":"English uses \"hello\" with almost everyone. Polish makes a clear choice: Dzień dobry (formal) for strangers, shops, offices and older people, Cześć (informal) for friends, family and children. Choosing the wrong one is the first thing people notice, so learn the pairs together. To say your name, Polish does not say \"my name is\": it says Mam na imię Anna (\"I have the name Anna\") or simply Jestem Anna. Key phrases Saying hello Polish English Dzień dobry. Good morning. / Good afternoon. / Hello. (formal, until evening) Dobry wieczór. Good evening. (formal) Cześć! Hi! (informal) Hej! Hey! (informal) Saying who you are Polish English Mam na imię Anna. My name is Anna. (first name) Nazywam się Anna Nowak. My name is Anna Nowak. (full name, formal) Jestem Tom. I'm Tom. Jak masz na imię? What's your name? (informal) Jak się pan nazywa? / Jak się pani nazywa? What's your name? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Miło mi. / Bardzo mi miło. Nice to meet you. Asking how someone is Polish English Jak się masz? How are you? (informal) Jak się pan ma? / Jak się pani ma? How are you? (formal, to a man / to a woman) Dobrze, dziękuję. A ty? Fine, thanks. And you? (informal) Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? / A pani? Fine, thank you. And you? (formal) W porządku. All right. / OK. Saying goodbye Polish English Do widzenia. Goodbye. (formal) Cześć! / Na razie! Bye! / See you! (informal) Do zobaczenia. See you. (neutral, also formal) Do jutra! See you tomorrow! Dobranoc. Good night. (late, when you leave or go to bed) Cześć works both ways: hello and goodbye. Dialogue Anna meets her neighbour, pan Adam, outside their block of flats. Her friend Piotr arrives. Anna: Dzień dobry! (Hello!) Pan Adam: Dzień dobry! Jak się pani ma? (Hello! How are you?) Anna: Dobrze, dziękuję. A pan? (Fine, thank you. And you?) Pan Adam: W porządku, dziękuję. (All right, thank you.) Piotr: Cześć, Ania! (Hi, Ania!) Anna: Cześć, Piotrek! To jest pan Adam Nowak. (Hi, Piotrek! This is Mr Adam Nowak.) Piotr: Dzień dobry. Nazywam się Piotr Wójcik. Miło mi. (Hello. My name is Piotr Wójcik. Nice to meet you.) Pan Adam: Bardzo mi miło. Do widzenia! (Very nice to meet you. Goodbye!) Anna: Do widzenia! (Goodbye!) Anna and Piotr are friends, so they say Cześć. With pan Adam both use Dzień dobry and Do widzenia. Culture note Say Dzień dobry when you walk into a small shop, a doctor's waiting room or a lift, and Do widzenia when you leave. Neighbours and colleagues often use pan or pani with a first name: pan Adam, pani Ewa. It is polite but friendly. Friends use short forms of names: Anna → Ania, Anka; Piotr → Piotrek; Katarzyna → Kasia. People usually shake hands when they meet for the first time. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Dobry dzień! → ✓ Dzień dobry! — a fixed phrase; the word order is not the English \"good day\". ✗ Moje imię jest Tom. → ✓ Mam na imię Tom. — Polish says \"I have the name\", or just Jestem Tom. ✗ Jak się jesteś? → ✓ Jak się masz? — the phrase uses masz (\"have\"), not \"are\". ✗ Cześć! to a shop assistant → ✓ Dzień dobry! — cześć is for friends, family and people your age in informal settings. ✗ Dziekuje. → ✓ Dziękuję. — the marks are part of the spelling. Practice Formal or informal? Choose the right greeting. You meet your friend Kasia: Dzień dobry or Cześć? You walk into a bakery: Dzień dobry or Cześć? You leave a doctor's office: Do widzenia or Na razie? Complete the phrase. Mam imię Marek. (My name is Marek.) Bardzo mi . (Very nice to meet you.) Translate into Polish. How are you? (to a friend) How are you? (formal, to a woman) Good evening. (formal) Answers Cześć Dzień dobry Do widzenia na miło Jak się masz? Jak się pani ma? Dobry wieczór.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-greetings-introductions"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani","group":"pages","title":"Personal pronouns and formal address","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"ja, ty, on, ona, ono, my, wy, oni, one, and the polite pan, pani, państwo for \"you\". Jak się pan ma?","text":"English has one \"you\" for everyone. Polish has ty (one person) and wy (several people) for friends, family and children, and pan (to a man), pani (to a woman), państwo (to a group) for everyone else. The polite words really mean \"sir\", \"madam\" and \"ladies and gentlemen\", so the verb after them is the \"he / she / they\" form. Polish also has two words for \"they\", and often leaves pronouns out. How it works Person Singular Plural 1st ja (I) my (we) 2nd, informal ty (you, one friend) wy (you, several friends) 3rd on (he), ona (she), ono (it) oni (they: a group with at least one man), one (they: all other groups) Formal \"you\": Talking to Polish Verb form a man pan like on: Jak się pan ma? a woman pani like ona: Jak się pani ma? a mixed group, or a couple państwo like oni: Jak się państwo mają? a group of men / of women panowie / panie like oni / one ono is for neuter nouns, for example dziecko (child). Things are usually on or ona: a phone (telefon) is on, a lamp (lampa) is ona: see The three genders of nouns . When to use it Usually leave the pronoun out. The verb ending already says who: Jak się masz? (How are you?), not Jak się ty masz? Use it for contrast or emphasis. Ja jestem Ewa, a ty? (I'm Ewa, and you?) Use ty with friends, family and children. Cześć, Ola! Jak się masz? (Hi, Ola! How are you?) Use pan / pani with strangers, in shops, at work and with older people. Dzień dobry. Jak się pani ma? (Hello. How are you, madam?) Use oni if the group has at least one man, one if it has none. Ewa i Marek? Oni są tu. (Ewa and Marek? They're here.) Examples Polish English Jak się pan ma? How are you? (to a man) Dobrze, dziękuję. A pani? Fine, thank you. And you? (to a woman) Dzień dobry! Jak się państwo mają? Hello! How are you? (formal, to a couple or a group) Cześć! Jak się macie? Hi! How are you? (to several friends) Kasia? Ona jest tu. Kasia? She's here. Ania i Ola? One są tam. Ania and Ola? They're over there. Piotr i Ewa? Oni są w domu. Piotr and Ewa? They're at home. My jesteśmy z Gdańska, a wy? We're from Gdańsk, and you? jest (is) and są (are) come from być (to be): być (to be) and to jest / to są . Good to know Proszę pana / proszę pani is how you politely get someone's attention: \"Excuse me, sir / madam\". Moving from pan / pani to ty is a social step; usually the older person offers it. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Jak się pan masz? → ✓ Jak się pan ma? — pan takes the \"he\" form of the verb, not the \"you\" form. ✗ Jak się masz? to a stranger → ✓ Jak się pan ma? / Jak się pani ma? — ty to strangers sounds rude or too familiar. ✗ Jak się macie? to one older person → ✓ Jak się pani ma? — wy is never the polite form for one person. ✗ Anna i Ewa? Oni są tu. → ✓ Anna i Ewa? One są tu. — oni needs at least one man in the group. ✗ Ja jestem Tom. Ja jestem tu. → ✓ Jestem Tom. Jestem tu. — the verb shows the person; repeating ja sounds heavy. Practice Which pronoun? Replace the name or names. Marek → Kasia i Ola → Kasia i Marek → dziecko → Formal or informal? Choose. To a friend: A ty? or A pan? To an older woman you don't know: Jak się masz? or Jak się pani ma? Translate into Polish. How are you? (to a group of friends) And you? (formal, to a man) Answers on one oni ono A ty? Jak się pani ma? Jak się macie? A pan?","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-pronouns-pan-pani"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-byc","group":"pages","title":"być (to be) and to jest / to są","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The forms of być (jestem, jesteś, jest, jesteśmy, jesteście, są) and pointing at things with to jest and to są.","text":"Być is \"to be\". Like English \"am / is / are\", it is irregular, so learn its six forms by heart. Two things are different from English: the ending already says who, so Jestem Tom is a full sentence without \"I\", and \"this is / these are\" is to jest / to są, where to never changes, whatever you point at. How it works Present tense of być: Person Singular Plural 1st (ja) jestem (I am) (my) jesteśmy (we are) 2nd (ty) jesteś (you are) (wy) jesteście (you are) 3rd (on, ona, ono) jest (he / she / it is) (oni, one) są (they are) formal pan / pani jest państwo są Pointing: Polish English Rule To jest Marek. This is Marek. one person or thing: to jest To są moje dzieci. These are my children. more than one: to są Kto to jest? Who is this? people and animals Co to jest? What is this? things Negative: put nie in front: nie jestem, to nie jest. When to use it Saying who you are: Jestem Ola. (I'm Ola.) Describing: Jesteś bardzo miły. (You're very kind. — to a man; to a woman: miła.) Saying where someone is: Gdzie jesteś? — Jestem tu. (Where are you? — I'm here.) Introducing and pointing: To jest pani Nowak. (This is Mrs Nowak.) To są moje siostry. (These are my sisters.) Asking: Czy to jest twój telefon? (Is this your phone?) — czy at the start turns a statement into a yes / no question. Examples Polish English Jestem Kuba, a to jest Zosia. I'm Kuba, and this is Zosia. Gdzie jesteście? — Jesteśmy tu! Where are you? — We're here! Pan jest bardzo miły. You are very kind. (to a man) Kasia i Marek są tam. Kasia and Marek are over there. Co to jest? — To jest herbata. What's this? — It's tea. To są moje dzieci, Antek i Zuzia. These are my children, Antek and Zuzia. Nie jestem zmęczona. I'm not tired. (a woman speaking) Kto to jest? — To jest mój brat. Who's this? — It's my brother. Good to know The adjective after jestem shows your gender: a man says jestem zmęczony, a woman jestem zmęczona. For a job or nationality (I'm a student, she's a doctor) the noun needs a new ending after a form of być (jestem, jest…): Instrumental after być and with z . After to jest the noun does not change: To jest student. Age uses mieć (to have), not być: Mam trzydzieści lat (I'm thirty). See Give and ask for personal details . Tu and tutaj both mean \"here\"; tam is \"there\". Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Oni jest tu. → ✓ Oni są tu. — the plural \"they are\" is są. ✗ To są mój brat. → ✓ To jest mój brat. — one person: to jest. ✗ To jest moje dzieci. → ✓ To są moje dzieci. — dzieci is plural, so to są. ✗ Jestem trzydzieści. → ✓ Mam trzydzieści lat. — age uses mieć, not być. ✗ Ja jestem Tom, ja jestem tu. → ✓ Jestem Tom, jestem tu. — jestem already means \"I am\". Practice Complete with the right form of być. My tu. Ty Ola? Oni w domu. Pan bardzo miły. Wy tam? To jest or to są? moja siostra. moje dzieci. Translate into Polish. Where are you? (to one friend) Answers jesteśmy jesteś są jest jesteście To jest To są Gdzie jesteś?","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-byc"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-numbers-days-months","group":"pages","title":"Numbers 0–100, days of the week, months","headings":["Words","Word partners","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Count from zero to a hundred, name the days and months, and say \"on Monday\" and \"in July\". Days and months take small letters.","text":"Polish numbers are built like English ones: dwadzieścia (twenty) + dwa (two) = dwadzieścia dwa (twenty-two), with no \"and\" and no hyphen. Two differences matter from day one: days and months are written with a small letter, and \"on Monday\" or \"in July\" change the end of the word: poniedziałek → w poniedziałek, lipiec → w lipcu. Learn these short phrases as they are; the rules behind them come later. Words Numbers 0–20 Polish English zero 0 jeden 1 dwa 2 trzy 3 cztery 4 pięć 5 sześć 6 siedem 7 osiem 8 dziewięć 9 dziesięć 10 jedenaście 11 dwanaście 12 trzynaście 13 czternaście 14 piętnaście 15 szesnaście 16 siedemnaście 17 osiemnaście 18 dziewiętnaście 19 dwadzieścia 20 Tens and a hundred Polish English trzydzieści 30 czterdzieści 40 pięćdziesiąt 50 sześćdziesiąt 60 siedemdziesiąt 70 osiemdziesiąt 80 dziewięćdziesiąt 90 sto 100 Days of the week Polish English on … poniedziałek (m) Monday w poniedziałek wtorek (m) Tuesday we wtorek środa (f) Wednesday w środę czwartek (m) Thursday w czwartek piątek (m) Friday w piątek sobota (f) Saturday w sobotę niedziela (f) Sunday w niedzielę Months Polish English in … styczeń (m) January w styczniu luty (m) February w lutym marzec (m) March w marcu kwiecień (m) April w kwietniu maj (m) May w maju czerwiec (m) June w czerwcu lipiec (m) July w lipcu sierpień (m) August w sierpniu wrzesień (m) September we wrześniu październik (m) October w październiku listopad (m) November w listopadzie grudzień (m) December w grudniu Word partners Polish English Jaki dzisiaj jest dzień? What day is it today? Dzisiaj jest środa. Today is Wednesday. Jutro jest sobota. Tomorrow is Saturday. w sobotę i w niedzielę on Saturday and Sunday w weekend at the weekend we wtorek rano on Tuesday morning w lipcu i w sierpniu in July and August Mój numer to sześć, zero, jeden… My number is six, zero, one… autobus numer dwadzieścia trzy bus number twenty-three pokój numer czternaście room fourteen Ile? — Dwanaście. How many? — Twelve. Good to know Phone numbers can be read digit by digit: sześć, dwa, zero… Poles often read them in groups of three, which needs the hundreds (A2). we instead of w before w or f followed by another consonant: we wtorek, we wrześniu. It is easier to say. Jeden changes with gender (jedna kawa, jedno piwo), and dwa becomes dwie with feminine nouns (dwie kawy). Counting things comes in Order in a café and buy things . Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ w Poniedziałek → ✓ w poniedziałek — days and months take a small letter in Polish. ✗ w sobota → ✓ w sobotę — \"on\" + a day changes the ending: -a → -ę. ✗ pięćnaście → ✓ piętnaście — 15 and 19 are spelled with t: piętnaście, dziewiętnaście. ✗ dwadzieścia i dwa → ✓ dwadzieścia dwa — no \"and\" inside a number. ✗ w lipiec → ✓ w lipcu — \"in\" + a month changes the ending; learn the w … forms as phrases. Practice Write the numbers in words. 17 45 99 Which day comes next? poniedziałek, wtorek, piątek, sobota, Complete with the right phrase. \"on Saturday\" = w \"in May\" = w Translate into Polish. Today is Thursday. Answers siedemnaście czterdzieści pięć dziewięćdziesiąt dziewięć środa niedziela sobotę maju Dzisiaj jest czwartek. / Dziś jest czwartek.","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-numbers-days-months"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-gender-of-nouns","group":"pages","title":"The three genders of nouns","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"ten dom, ta kawa, to okno","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-gender-of-nouns"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-nominative","group":"pages","title":"Nominative: naming things; adjective agreement","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Kto to jest? Co to jest? nowy dom, nowa praca, nowe auto","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-nominative"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-instrumental-identity","group":"pages","title":"Instrumental after być and with z","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Jestem studentem. Ona jest lekarką. kawa z mlekiem","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-instrumental-identity"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-personal-details","group":"pages","title":"Give and ask for personal details","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Skąd jesteś? Jestem z Kanady. Mam 30 lat. Mieszkam w Krakowie.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-personal-details"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-family-people","group":"pages","title":"Family, describing people, nationalities, jobs","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"mój brat jest wysoki; jestem Amerykaninem / Amerykanką","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-family-people"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-present-conjugations","group":"pages","title":"Present tense: the three conjugation patterns","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"czytam, czytasz; mówię, mówisz; piszę, piszesz; pracuję","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-present-conjugations"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-common-irregular","group":"pages","title":"mieć and other common irregular verbs","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"mam, idę, jadę, jem, wiem, chcę, mogę","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-common-irregular"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-accusative","group":"pages","title":"Accusative: the direct object","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes for English speakers","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"The form for the thing or person an action goes to. Mam brata. Lubię kawę.","text":"English shows who does what by word order: \"Anna loves Tom\" is not \"Tom loves Anna\". Polish shows it with endings. The person or thing the action goes to (the direct object) takes the accusative (biernik, questions: kogo? co?). Mam brata. Lubię kawę. The good news: for many nouns the accusative is the same as the dictionary form. How it works Singular. The plural comes at A2: Accusative plural and masculine personal accusative . Gender Noun Adjective Nominative → accusative masculine: people and animals -a -ego dobry brat → Mam dobrego brata. masculine: things no change no change nowy telefon → Mam nowy telefon. feminine -a → -ę -a → -ą dobra kawa → Piję dobrą kawę. neuter no change no change małe piwo → Poproszę małe piwo. So only two groups really change: masculine nouns for people and animals, and feminine nouns. When to use it After most verbs that take an object: mieć, lubić, znać, czytać, pić, jeść, kupować, oglądać, kochać. Czytam książkę. (I'm reading a book.) When you order or ask for something: Poproszę kawę i wodę. (A coffee and a water, please.) With days of the week after w: w środę (on Wednesday), w sobotę (on Saturday). Not after nie. A negated object takes the genitive: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu. See Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions . Examples Polish English Mam brata i siostrę. I have a brother and a sister. Lubię polską kuchnię. I like Polish food. Czytasz książkę czy gazetę? Are you reading a book or a newspaper? Poproszę herbatę z cytryną. A tea with lemon, please. Anna ma małego psa. Anna has a small dog. Kupuję nowy telefon. I'm buying a new phone. Jem kanapkę i piję sok. I'm eating a sandwich and drinking juice. Znasz mojego kolegę? Do you know my friend? Good to know Masculine nouns that end in -a (kolega, tata, mężczyzna) take -ę like feminine nouns, but their adjectives stay masculine: Znam twojego tatę. pani becomes panią: Przepraszam panią. (Excuse me, ma'am.) Some nouns lose an e before the ending: pies → psa, ojciec → ojca. Common mistakes for English speakers ✗ Lubię kawa. → ✓ Lubię kawę. — the object needs its ending; word order does not mark it in Polish. ✗ Mam brat. → ✓ Mam brata. — masculine people and animals take -a. ✗ Piję dobra kawę. → ✓ Piję dobrą kawę. — the adjective changes too: -a → -ą. ✗ Kupuję nowego samochoda. → ✓ Kupuję nowy samochód. — masculine things do not change. ✗ To jest kawę. → ✓ To jest kawa. — after to jest use the nominative. Practice Put the words in brackets into the accusative. Mam (siostra) . Lubisz (herbata) ? Poproszę (zupa pomidorowa) . Tomek ma (kot) . Czytam (ciekawa książka) . Mamy (nowy nauczyciel) . Kupuję (mleko) i (chleb) . Translate into Polish. I have a brother. Answers siostrę herbatę zupę pomidorową kota ciekawą książkę nowego nauczyciela mleko, chleb Mam brata.","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-accusative"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-questions-negation","group":"pages","title":"Questions and negation","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"czy, co, kto, gdzie, kiedy, jak, ile, dlaczego; nie rozumiem","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-questions-negation"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-food-drink","group":"pages","title":"Food and drink","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"chleb, ser, woda, herbata z cytryną, śniadanie, obiad","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-food-drink"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-genitive-basics","group":"pages","title":"Genitive: negation, possession and common prepositions","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Nie mam czasu. To jest dom brata. do, z, od, bez, dla","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-genitive-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-modal-verbs-basics","group":"pages","title":"lubić, chcieć, móc, musieć, umieć + infinitive","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Lubię czytać. Chcę spać. Czy mogę zapłacić kartą? Muszę iść.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-modal-verbs-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-ordering-shopping","group":"pages","title":"Order in a café and buy things","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Poproszę kawę. Ile to kosztuje? Czy mogę prosić o rachunek?","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-ordering-shopping"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-time-dates","group":"pages","title":"Tell the time and say when things happen","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Jest trzecia. O której? O ósmej. W sobotę wieczorem.","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-time-dates"},{"id":"page:a1-vocabulary-home-city","group":"pages","title":"Home, furniture and places in town","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"kuchnia, łazienka, sklep, apteka, dworzec, przystanek","level":"A1","category":"Vocabulary","route":"#/a1/vocabulary-home-city"},{"id":"page:a1-cases-locative-places","group":"pages","title":"Locative: saying where you are","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"w Krakowie, w domu, na uniwersytecie, w pracy, w Polsce","level":"A1","category":"Cases","route":"#/a1/cases-locative-places"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-going-isc-jechac","group":"pages","title":"Going places: iść and jechać","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Idę do sklepu. Jadę do Warszawy. Idę na koncert.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-going-isc-jechac"},{"id":"page:a1-grammar-possessives-demonstratives","group":"pages","title":"Possessives and demonstratives","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"mój, twój, jego, jej, nasz, wasz, ich; ten, ta, to","level":"A1","category":"Grammar","route":"#/a1/grammar-possessives-demonstratives"},{"id":"page:a1-verbs-past-tense-basics","group":"pages","title":"Past tense: first forms","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"byłem / byłam, miałem / miałam, robiłem / robiłam","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: aspect and tenses","route":"#/a1/verbs-past-tense-basics"},{"id":"page:a1-functions-asking-to-repeat","group":"pages","title":"Survival phrases: ask people to repeat, slow down, explain","headings":[],"summary":"This topic is being written.","text":"Proszę powtórzyć. Proszę mówić wolniej. Nie rozumiem. Jak to się mówi po polsku?","level":"A1","category":"Communication","route":"#/a1/functions-asking-to-repeat"},{"id":"cards","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: flashcards from decks, My mistakes and weak spots from practice, at growing intervals.","text":"flashcards review active recall decks mistake log weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"cards:mistakes","group":"exercises","title":"My mistakes (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"A flashcard deck made from the entries of the mistake log.","text":"flashcards my mistakes mistake log","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/u-mistakes"},{"id":"cards:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with the sentence and the meaning.","text":"my words personal glossary saved words flashcards selection","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"cards:deck:case-accusative","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative in sentences (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 6 cards. The full deck has 30 cards. Put the phrase in brackets into the accusative.","text":"flashcards deck Accusative in sentences A1-A2","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/case-accusative"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-1","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, find the mistake and translate.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-1"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-2","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: type it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the forms, write a dictation, rewrite sentences, find the mistake and translate.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-2"},{"id":"set:a1-cases-accusative-3","group":"exercises","title":"Accusative: listen, rewrite, fill the table","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: two dictations, short answers, rewriting, a table of forms, pairs and a translation.","text":"Topic: Accusative: the direct object","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-cases-accusative-3"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1","group":"exercises","title":"Sounds and stress: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: find the stress, match letters and sounds, add the marks and write a dictation.","text":"Topic: Alphabet, sounds and stress","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-alphabet-pronunciation-1"},{"id":"set:a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani-1","group":"exercises","title":"Pronouns and pan / pani: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the pronoun, fill the table, pick the polite form and correct the mistakes.","text":"Topic: Personal pronouns and formal address","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-grammar-pronouns-pan-pani-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-byc-1","group":"exercises","title":"być: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: conjugate być, choose to jest or to są, correct the mistakes and translate.","text":"Topic: być (to be) and to jest / to są","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-byc-1"},{"id":"mix:mix-a1-u1","group":"exercises","title":"Unit review: Sounds and first words","headings":[],"summary":"Sounds and stress, pronouns and pan / pani, and być, mixed.","text":"Mixed set. Topics: Alphabet, sounds and stress, Personal pronouns and formal address, być (to be) and to jest / to są","level":null,"category":"Mixed set","route":"#/practice/mix/mix-a1-u1"},{"id":"lab","group":"lab","title":"Case Lab","headings":[],"summary":"Seven Polish cases on cards, Pick the case, Compare, Declension and the aspect strip.","text":"cases case lab declension endings nominative genitive dative accusative instrumental locative vocative aspect","level":null,"category":"Case Lab","route":"#/lab"},{"id":"lab:nominative","group":"lab","title":"Nominative (Mianownik)","headings":["Subject","After to jest / to są","Adjective after być","Name after nazywać się"],"summary":"The dictionary form: the subject of the sentence, and the noun after to jest / to są.","text":"kto? co? Anna pracuje w banku. Anna works at a bank. To jest mój brat. This is my brother. Kawa jest gorąca. The coffee is hot. Nazywam się Tomek. My name is Tomek. być + adjective nazywać się to jest to są After być a noun goes into the instrumental: Jestem studentem, not Jestem student. With to jest the noun stays in the nominative: To jest student. Masculine personal plurals change the last consonant: student → studenci, Polak → Polacy. Not studenty, Polaki. The subject is in the nominative. The direct object is in the accusative: feminine nouns change -a to -ę. Kawa jest dobra. Lubię kawę.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/nominative"},{"id":"lab:genitive","group":"lab","title":"Genitive (Dopełniacz)","headings":["After negation","Possession (of whom?)","Quantity and containers","After do, z, bez, dla, u"],"summary":"Absence, possession, quantity, and after many prepositions.","text":"kogo? czego? Nie mam czasu. I don't have time. To jest samochód Marka. This is Marek's car. Poproszę butelkę wody. A bottle of water, please. Idę do sklepu. I'm going to the shop. do z (from) od bez dla u obok koło szukać słuchać używać bać się potrzebować nie ma nie mam, nie lubię … dużo mało kilka pięć i więcej English does not change the object after not, so learners keep the accusative: Nie mam czas ✗ → Nie mam czasu ✓. Masculine people and animals that end in a consonant always take -a (brata, kota). For things it is -a or -u, and you learn them in pairs (dom – domu, chleb – chleba). After nie only an object that would be in the accusative changes. The subject stays in the nominative (Kawa nie jest droga), and other cases stay too (Nie pomagam mamie). A positive sentence takes the accusative. The same sentence with nie takes the genitive. Mam samochód. Nie mam samochodu. do + genitive = where to (a shop, a city). w + locative = where you are. Jadę do Krakowa. Mieszkam w Krakowie.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/genitive"},{"id":"lab:accusative","group":"lab","title":"Accusative (Biernik)","headings":["Direct object","Ordering","Where to, after na","Days of the week"],"summary":"The direct object: the person or thing the action goes to.","text":"kogo? co? Czytam książkę. I'm reading a book. Poproszę kawę. A coffee, please. Idę na koncert. I'm going to a concert. w sobotę on Saturday na (where to) w (days: w sobotę) przez za (in … time) o (prosić o, pytać o) mieć lubić znać widzieć czytać pić jeść kupować kochać odwiedzać English marks the object only by its place in the sentence. Polish needs the ending: Lubię kawę, not Lubię kawa. Masculine people and animals take -a, like the genitive: Mam brata, Mam psa. Things do not change: Mam dom. After nie the object switches to the genitive: Mam czas. → Nie mam czasu. Going into a building or a town takes do + genitive, not w + accusative: Idę do szkoły, not Idę w szkołę. Positive sentence: accusative. Negated sentence: genitive. Mam samochód. Nie mam samochodu. A feminine noun ends in -a as the subject and in -ę as the object. Książka jest ciekawa. Czytam książkę. na + accusative = where to. na + locative = where. With w, where to is usually do + genitive: Idę do szkoły, Jestem w szkole. Idę na pocztę. Jestem na poczcie.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · case card","route":"#/lab/accusative"},{"id":"lab:model:nowy-student","group":"lab","title":"nowy student – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"new student, masculine personal: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension nowy student nowego studenta nowemu studentowi nowym studentem nowym studencie nowy studencie nowi studenci nowych studentów nowym studentom nowymi studentami nowych studentach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/nowy-student"},{"id":"lab:model:stary-dom","group":"lab","title":"stary dom – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"old house, masculine inanimate: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension stary dom starego domu staremu domowi starym domem starym domu stary domu stare domy starych domów starym domom starymi domami starych domach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/stary-dom"},{"id":"lab:model:dobra-kawa","group":"lab","title":"dobra kawa – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"good coffee, feminine: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension dobra kawa dobrej kawy dobrej kawie dobrą kawę dobrą kawą dobra kawo dobre kawy dobrych kaw dobrym kawom dobrymi kawami dobrych kawach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/dobra-kawa"},{"id":"lab:model:male-okno","group":"lab","title":"małe okno – all forms","headings":[],"summary":"small window, neuter: 14 forms in the declension viewer.","text":"declension małe okno małego okna małemu oknu małym oknem małym oknie małe okna małych okien małym oknom małymi oknami małych oknach","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · declension","route":"#/lab/forms/model/male-okno"},{"id":"lab:tree:pick-case","group":"lab","title":"Pick the case","headings":[],"summary":"Think of one noun in your sentence and answer the questions about it.","text":"Is there a preposition right before the noun (or before its adjective)? Yes, there is a preposition   No preposition   Which preposition is it? do, od, bez, dla, u, obok, koło These prepositions always take the genitive. Idę do sklepu. z   na   w   przez, or za meaning in … time (za tydzień) przez and za meaning in … time take the accusative. (za meaning behind takes the instrumental: za domem.) Wracam za tydzień. o, po, przy (about, after, next to) o, po and przy usually take the locative: mówić o filmie, po lekcji, przy oknie. (o meaning for, as in prosić o, takes the accusative, and so does po meaning to fetch: Idę po chleb.) Rozmawiamy o pracy. przed, pod, nad, między (in front of, under, above, between) When they say where something is, these prepositions take the instrumental. (Going under or in front of something takes the accusative: Kot wchodzi pod stół.) Czekam przed domem. What does z mean here? from, out of (Where from?) z meaning from takes the genitive. Jestem z Polski. with, together with (With whom? With what?) z meaning with takes the instrumental. Piję kawę z mlekiem. Does na say where something goes, or where it is? Where it goes (Where to?) na + accusative = where to. Idę na koncert. Where it is (Where?) na + locative = where. Jestem na koncercie. What does w say here? Where something is (Where?) w + locative = where: in a building, a town, a country. Mieszkam w Krakowie. Where someone goes: into a building, a town, a country Going into a place usually takes do + genitive, not w: Idę do szkoły, not Idę w szkołę. Jadę do Warszawy. A day of the week (on Monday …) w + a day of the week takes the accusative. (Months and years take the locative: w maju.) Do zobaczenia w sobotę. A month or a year (in May, in 2025) w + a month or a year takes the locative. Mam urlop w maju. What does the noun do in the sentence? It is the subject: the person or thing that does the action The subject is in the nominative. (After quantity words like dużo or pięć it goes into the genitive: Pięć osób czeka.) Mój brat pracuje w Warszawie. It comes after to jest or to są After to jest / to są the noun stays in the nominative. To jest moja siostra. It is a noun after być saying who someone is (a job, a nationality, a role) być + noun takes the instrumental: Jestem lekarzem. With a first name in an introduction the nominative is usual: Jestem Tomek. An adjective after być stays in the nominative: Kawa jest gorąca. Ona jest nauczycielką. It is the object of the verb: the person or thing the action goes to   It says whose something is, or follows a quantity word (dużo, mało, pięć …) Possession and quantities take the genitive. Mam dużo pracy. It is the person who receives something (To whom?) The receiver of a gift, a message or help is in the dative. Daję mamie kwiaty. It is a tool or a means of transport (With what? By what?) Tools and means of transport without a preposition take the instrumental. Jadę autobusem. You are speaking to someone directly Addressing someone takes the vocative. Dzień dobry, panie profesorze! Which kind of verb is it? szukać, słuchać, potrzebować, używać, bać się These verbs take the genitive, also in a positive sentence. Szukam klucza. pomagać, dziękować, or the person after dawać (komu?) The person you help, thank or give something to is in the dative, also after nie. The thing you give stays in the accusative: Daję mamie kwiaty. Pomagam bratu. Another verb (mieć, lubić, kupować, czytać …)   Is the verb negated with nie? Yes, there is nie before the verb After nie the direct object switches from the accusative to the genitive. Nie lubię kawy. No, the sentence is positive Most verbs take a direct object in the accusative. Kupuję gazetę i kawę.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/pick-case"},{"id":"lab:aspect","group":"lab","title":"Aspect and tense on one timeline","headings":[],"summary":"Most Polish verbs come in pairs. The imperfective verb looks at the action as a process or a habit; the perfective verb looks at one finished result. Together they give five forms.","text":"aspect imperfective perfective tense czytać przeczytać Present imperfective czyta Ola czyta książkę. Ola is reading a book. Past imperfective czytała Wczoraj Ola czytała książkę przez dwie godziny. Yesterday Ola spent two hours reading a book. Past perfective przeczytała Ola już przeczytała tę książkę. Ola has already read this book. Future imperfective będzie czytać Jutro Ola będzie czytać cały wieczór. Tomorrow Ola will be reading all evening. Future perfective przeczyta Ola przeczyta tę książkę do piątku. Ola will have read this book by Friday.","level":null,"category":"Case Lab · aspect","route":"#/lab/aspect"}]};
